{"causes":["The established patrilineal succession within the Xia dynasty ensured Jiong's ascent to the throne after his father Buye's death.","The ongoing consolidation of power by the Xia kings, even if semi-legendary, created a framework for Jiong's rule.","The need for a continuous narrative of legitimate rule across generations influenced later chroniclers to include Jiong in the dynastic record."],"effects":["Jiong's reign contributed to the continuity of the Xia dynastic line, ensuring the succession of his son, Jin.","His inclusion in traditional histories reinforced the concept of hereditary monarchy as a foundational element of Chinese governance.","The narrative of Jiong's rule helped solidify the legendary framework upon which later, more historically verifiable Chinese dynasties were built."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"buye-of-xia","sourceName":"Buye of Xia","verb":"SUCCEEDS","targetSlug":"jiong-of-xia","targetName":"Jiong of Xia","context":"Jiong of Xia succeeded his father, Buye of Xia, as the twelfth king of the Xia dynasty."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"jin-of-xia","targetName":"Jin of Xia","context":"Jiong of Xia's reign directly led to the succession of his son, Jin of Xia, maintaining the dynastic line."},{"sourceSlug":"jiong-of-xia","sourceName":"Jiong of Xia","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"yellow-river-valley","targetName":"Yellow River Valley","context":"The legendary reign of Jiong of Xia is traditionally placed within the geographical and cultural sphere of the Yellow River Valley in ancient China."},{"sourceSlug":"records-of-the-grand-historian","sourceName":"Records of the Grand Historian","verb":"CANONIZES","targetSlug":"jiong-of-xia","targetName":"Jiong of Xia","context":"Sima Qian's *Records of the Grand Historian*, compiled around 94 BCE, canonizes Jiong of Xia as a legitimate ruler within the Xia dynastic lineage."},{"sourceSlug":"xia-dynasty","sourceName":"Xia Dynasty","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"jiong-of-xia","targetName":"Jiong of Xia","context":"Jiong of Xia is recorded as the twelfth king within the traditional succession of the Xia Dynasty, a semi-legendary period of early Chinese history."}],"places":[{"name":"Erlitou, China","role":"Potential Capital Region"},{"name":"Yellow River Valley, China","role":"Cultural Hearth"},{"name":"Shanxi Province, China","role":"Traditional Domain"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Jiong of Xia","type":"Reference article","slug":"jiong-of-xia"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q780446","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiong_of_Xia"],"tags":["Monarchs & Rulers","political-systems","empire-and-colonialism","geopolitical-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Jiong of Xia (also written Jiong; fl. c. 2000 BCE, traditional chronology) was a legendary ruler in the traditional Chinese historical sequence of the Xia dynasty — the first dynasty in Chinese historical tradition, whose historicity has been a subject of scholarly debate between those who accept the traditional accounts and those who regard the Xia as legendary. In the traditional chronology preserved in the 'Records of the Grand Historian' (Shiji) by Sima Qian (c. 100 BCE), Jiong appears as one of the later rulers of the Xia, succeeding in the dynastic line that traces back to the legendary founder Yu the Great, who is credited with taming the great floods of ancient China.

The Xia dynasty (traditionally c. 2070–1600 BCE) occupies a crucial position in Chinese historical memory: it is the dynasty that bridges the era of legendary sage-rulers (Yao, Shun, and Yu) and the historically more documented Shang dynasty (c. 1600–1046 BCE). Whether the Xia dynasty as described in Chinese historical texts represents an actual Bronze Age polity, a legendary confederation of Neolithic cultures, or a later invention of Zhou-era historiography remains one of the central debates in Chinese archaeology. The discovery and excavation of Erlitou culture sites (c. 1900–1500 BCE) in the Yellow River valley — with their palace complexes, bronze artefacts, and signs of political centralisation — has led many scholars to identify Erlitou as the Xia civilisation, though the identification remains contested.

Jiong of Xia, like most rulers in the traditional Xia king list, is a name without independently datable documentation — his existence depends on the accuracy of the traditional historical lists transmitted through Chinese court historiography. His significance lies primarily as an entry in the record of China's claimed dynastic continuity — the unbroken sequence of dynasties from Xia through Shang, Zhou, and beyond that was central to Chinese political legitimacy and historical consciousness.","subjects":["Ancient China","Chinese Dynasties","Classical Era","Bronze Age","Chinese History","Ancient History","Xia Dynasty","Chinese Historiography"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":5,"significanceNarrative":"Jiong of Xia holds significance as a named link in the foundational, albeit semi-legendary, Xia dynastic narrative, contributing to the historical continuity and ideological legitimacy of subsequent Chinese imperial rule. His inclusion in later chronicles underscores the importance of an unbroken line of succession in early Chinese political thought.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
